; Nuke — the token layer.
;
; This file is a fragment. A grammar of Nuke is this file followed by a syntax
; layer, read in that order, and the assembly is what is normative — the way an
; ABNF specification includes RFC 5234's core rules by reference rather than
; restating them. `grammar/canonical.abnf` is the canonical form's syntax layer.
;
; The token layer is written once because both languages spell a token the same
; way. Nothing here may be redefined by a syntax layer: a second definition of a
; rule would silently replace this one.
;
; It is written in ABNF (RFC 5234) with the case-sensitive string literals of
; RFC 7405. ABNF strings are case-insensitive by default, so a plain "e" would
; admit `1E5` and a plain "n" would admit `\N`.
;
; Tokens are matched greedily: at any position a token extends as far as it can.
; Whitespace is therefore never required between two values, only between two
; values that would otherwise run together into one token — `[12]` holds one
; element and `[1 2]` holds two.
;
; `ows` marks every position where whitespace and comments are permitted; a
; syntax layer permits them nowhere else.

atom       = upper *( ALPHA / DIGIT )
ident      = lower *( lower / DIGIT / "_" )

string     = DQUOTE *char DQUOTE
char       = unescaped / escape
unescaped  = %x20-21 / %x23-5B / %x5D-10FFFF
escape     = %x5C ( DQUOTE / %x5C / %s"n" / %s"r" / %s"t" / unicode )
unicode    = %s"u{" 1*6hexdig %x7D

; A number token takes the whole run that could belong to a number, so that a
; misspelling is one bad token rather than two good ones: `1E5` is a single
; number and not the number 1 beside the atom E5, and `0xZZ` is one too rather
; than the number 0 beside a name. A token is well formed only if it also
; matches the rule refining it, which is where the one-spelling rules live — no
; leading zeros, lowercase `e`, no `+`, a digit either side of the point. Every
; syntax layer names its own refinement, and they no longer name the same one:
; only the surface language spells a number in a base other than ten.
;
; `dyadic-run` stands ahead of `decimal-run` because an alternation is ordered
; once translated and `0e5` has to reach the second. Both take the point, so a
; number with no fraction to its name still swallows one and says so.

number           = [ "-" ] ( dyadic-run / decimal-run )
decimal-run      = 1*DIGIT [ frac ] [ exp ]
dyadic-run       = "0" marker *( ALPHA / DIGIT ) [ frac ]
marker           = %s"b" / %s"q" / %s"o" / %s"x"
frac             = "." *DIGIT
exp              = ( %s"e" / %s"E" ) [ "-" / "+" ] *DIGIT

canonical-number = [ "-" ] uint [ canonical-frac ] [ canonical-exp ]
canonical-frac   = "." 1*DIGIT
canonical-exp    = %s"e" [ "-" ] uint
uint             = "0" / ( %x31-39 *DIGIT )

ows        = *( wschar / comment )
comment    = "#" *( %x09 / %x20-10FFFF )
wschar     = %x20 / %x09 / %x0A

; ---- character classes ------------------------------------------------------

upper      = %x41-5A
lower      = %x61-7A
hexdig     = DIGIT / %x41-46

; ---- core rules (RFC 5234 appendix B) ---------------------------------------

ALPHA      = %x41-5A / %x61-7A
DIGIT      = %x30-39
DQUOTE     = %x22
